## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit bit packing, non-overlapping fields, and in-place updates without loops. It exercises careful mask calculation and cross-boundary writes.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and clearing specific ranges
- Cross-boundary placement across lo/hi
- Safe in-place updates without branching
- Endianness-agnostic bit numbering

## Code Example
```c
void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFF) | (((uint64_t)0xFFF) << 8) | (((uint64_t)0xFFFF) << 32);
  uint64_t hi_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFFFFFFFF) << 16;
  *out_lo = (lo & ~lo_mask) | (uint64_t)A | (((uint64_t)B) << 8) | (((uint64_t)C) << 32);
  *out_hi = (hi & ~hi_mask) | (((uint64_t)D) << 16);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if ranges could overlap and you needed deterministic behavior?
- How would you validate correctness across all inputs in a unit test?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of multi-limb bit tricks and how two's complement generalizes to 128 bits without branches.

## Key Concepts

- x & (-x) principle for lsb isolation across 2 limbs
- careful carry propagation from low to high limb
- avoiding branches by using arithmetic comparisons that yield 0/1

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = ~x.lo + 1;
  uint64_t carry = (loNeg < ~x.lo);
  uint64_t hiNeg = ~x.hi + carry;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  r.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to also return the index of the isolated bit in a 0..127 range?
- What are the edge-case behaviors when x is zero or has a single 1 at the MSB?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about 64-bit field layouts and perform cross-field moves without branches, a common pattern in graphics and encoding pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masking for 16-bit lanes
- Cross-lane permutation with shifts
- No loops/branches; constant-time operations

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t swapR_B(uint64_t v) {
  uint64_t r = v & 0x000000000000FFFFULL;
  uint64_t g = v & 0x00000000FFFF0000ULL;
  uint64_t b = v & 0x0000FFFF00000000ULL;
  uint64_t a = v & 0xFFFF000000000000ULL;
  return (r << 32) | g | (b >> 32) | a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to swap G and A as well? 
- Analyze performance implications on 64-bit architectures with compiler optimizations.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform multi-word arithmetic with pure bitwise/arith operations, handling carry without branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit arithmetic via two 64-bit halves
- Carry propagation using unsigned overflow properties
- Branchless computation

## Code Example
```c
void add128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  uint64_t lo = a_lo + b_lo;
  uint64_t carry = (lo < a_lo);
  uint64_t hi = a_hi + b_hi + carry;
  *out_hi = hi;
  *out_lo = lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to 256-bit operands?
- What are the potential pitfalls on platforms with different endianness?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests branchless 128-bit rotation using only shifts.

## Key Concepts
- View 128-bit value via 128-bit type
- Branchless rotation with shifts and modulo
- Correct unpacking into hi/lo

## Code Example
```c
void rotateRight128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned int k, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  unsigned int kk = k & 127;
  __uint128_t v = (((__uint128_t)hi) << 64) | lo;
  v = (v >> kk) | (v << ((128 - kk) % 128));
  *out_hi = (uint64_t)(v >> 64);
  *out_lo = (uint64_t)v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement without 128-bit type support?
- What are performance trade-offs for different compilers?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of bit-level data packing and word-level efficiency.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (dilating)
- Inline masks and shifts for 32-bit results
- Morton codes for spatial indexing

## Code Example
```javascript
uint32_t part1by1(uint16_t n) {
  uint32_t x = n;
  x &= 0x0000FFFF;
  x = (x | (x << 8)) & 0x00FF00FF;
  x = (x | (x << 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F;
  x = (x | (x << 2)) & 0x33333333;
  x = (x | (x << 1)) & 0x55555555;
  return x;
}
uint32_t morton2D(uint16_t x, uint16_t y) {
  return (part1by1(y) << 1) | part1by1(x);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 3D Morton codes (x,y,z) and 64-bit result? 
- How would you adapt for non-power-of-two bit widths or streaming data?
